DETAILED EXPLANATION — Concept 3: Append Data to a File

This program adds new content to the end of an existing file
without erasing what was already there.

- Header file: <fstream> provides ofstream for writing.

- Append mode: std::ios::app is passed as the second argument to
  the ofstream constructor. This tells the stream to move to the
  end of the file before every write, and never overwrite existing
  content — unlike the default output mode used in Concept 1,
  which truncates the file first.

- Error checking: if (!outputFile) catches cases where the file
  path is invalid or cannot be accessed for writing.

- Writing: the << operator appends the new line after all
  previously written content, since the write position is always
  forced to the end in append mode.

- Closing: close() flushes the buffered write to disk and releases
  the file handle.

Why this matters: std::ios::app is the standard way to add log
entries, records, or updates to a file over multiple program runs
without destroying earlier data.

